  "live_records": {
    "q-7555": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7555",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/533a1d7aaa5cc94ed737e665189d3722582e83c3b556bce13ec0d4d0c547ffc8.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=9,$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+4 & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{5},0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
