    "q-7536": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7536",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/299ca5fbab1351ab47dc2be6adac47b3ba3daf747e54c9b41042076cd2fee669.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5,$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{5},0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-7536-a",
