    "q-160269": {
      "dom_id": "step-q160269",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=2$",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+4 & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+2)^{2}=-2.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/e57f57f3e0cff36d9cc89a8889fa2f7fa5f4e8f9ceb5703c2d16f4ea16f38174.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
